---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import MistakeExhibits from '../../components/MistakeExhibits.astro'
import mistakes from '../../data/mistakes.json'
---

<Base
  title="The mistake museum · the kernels wing · Rudrite Kernels"
  description="Eight real Pallas failures, reproduced and captured: the failing code, the exact error text, the fix, and the rule that prevents each."
>
  <div class="ws-ground">
    <section class="wband">
      <div class="wrap wgrid">
        <span class="wtag">the museum<br />kernels wing</span>
        <div>
          <p class="mono-label serves">assigned reading in kernel-path chapter 11 · every error below was reproduced for real; six from the interpreter, two from a TPU compile on v6e-1 · <a class="wing-link" href="/mistakes">all wings</a></p>
          <h1 class="wh1">Meet every failure in a toy first.</h1>
          <p class="wlede">
            Each exhibit ran, failed, and was captured: the snippet, the verbatim error (paths shortened),
            the fix, and the rule that prevents it. The last two could only come from a real chip: they
            speak the Mosaic layer's vocabulary of VMEM budgets and the (8, 128) lattice, which is exactly
            why chapter 07 teaches you to read that layer. Read them before your first kernel and again
            after; the error messages start making sense the second time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <MistakeExhibits entries={mistakes} />
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .serves { margin-bottom: 1rem; letter-spacing: 0.04em; }
  .wing-link { color: var(--accent-ink); text-decoration: none; }
  .wing-link:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
</style>
